WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.629 --> 00:00:03.159
(orchestral music)

00:00:03.159 --> 00:00:05.111
- [Narrator] We're in Culpeper, Virginia,

00:00:05.111 --> 00:00:09.592
at this building, which
once belonged to the Feds.

00:00:09.592 --> 00:00:10.458
- [Narrator] On the surface,

00:00:10.458 --> 00:00:13.208
the purpose was they stored gold here.

00:00:13.208 --> 00:00:15.118
- [Narrator] Four billion dollars' worth

00:00:15.118 --> 00:00:17.305
of gold currency, to be exact.

00:00:17.305 --> 00:00:19.118
But that was just on the surface.

00:00:19.118 --> 00:00:20.187
- [Narrator] The real purpose of it

00:00:20.187 --> 00:00:23.735
was sort of an anti-nuclear
contingency program.

00:00:23.735 --> 00:00:24.758
- [Narrator] During the Cold War,

00:00:24.758 --> 00:00:25.907
if the big bomb went off,

00:00:25.907 --> 00:00:27.687
they would move the president here.

00:00:27.687 --> 00:00:29.407
But that's all in the past.

00:00:29.407 --> 00:00:32.927
Now they house something
completely different, film.

00:00:32.927 --> 00:00:34.638
- Yes, we have the classics here,

00:00:34.638 --> 00:00:36.067
we have Casablanca,

00:00:36.067 --> 00:00:37.856
and we have The Maltese Falcon,

00:00:37.856 --> 00:00:39.518
and we have Frankenstein.

00:00:39.518 --> 00:00:41.397
We also have Gigli,

00:00:41.397 --> 00:00:44.283
and we have all the Adam Sandler movies.

00:00:44.283 --> 00:00:45.427
- [Narrator] This building is now

00:00:45.427 --> 00:00:47.916
the Library of Congress's Packard Campus.

00:00:47.916 --> 00:00:50.646
Its goal is to ensure the
survival and conservation

00:00:50.646 --> 00:00:52.256
of our nation's films.

00:00:52.256 --> 00:00:55.456
And over 144,000 of the
most precious of these

00:00:55.456 --> 00:00:58.764
is under the care of George Willeman.

00:00:58.764 --> 00:01:00.542
(film leader beeping)
(projector whirring)

00:01:00.542 --> 00:01:02.974
But first, let's explore the building.

00:01:02.974 --> 00:01:04.753
- There is a suite

00:01:04.753 --> 00:01:07.934
where our technicians do
nothing but repair films.

00:01:07.934 --> 00:01:09.581
We have specialized rooms for printing,

00:01:09.581 --> 00:01:11.060
for film processing,

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:13.934
DataCine transfer, cylinder recordings,

00:01:13.934 --> 00:01:16.462
special suites for doing
two-inch videotapes,

00:01:16.462 --> 00:01:17.774
one-inch videotapes,

00:01:17.774 --> 00:01:19.392
three-quarter-inch videotape.

00:01:19.392 --> 00:01:20.902
You name it, we play it.

00:01:20.902 --> 00:01:22.190
- [Narrator] All these rooms exist

00:01:22.190 --> 00:01:23.622
because the Packard Campus

00:01:23.622 --> 00:01:26.732
is about restoration and preservation.

00:01:26.732 --> 00:01:29.061
And those first early pieces,

00:01:29.061 --> 00:01:30.649
they were made on nitrate.

00:01:30.649 --> 00:01:32.909
And that's what George is in charge of.

00:01:32.909 --> 00:01:35.758
He's head of the nitrate vaults.

00:01:35.758 --> 00:01:39.470
The oldest and most
dangerous of all the film bases.

00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:42.270
- We have 124 nitrate film vaults.

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:45.476
They're very austere-looking,
like a high-security prison.

00:01:45.476 --> 00:01:47.249
And they're very thick-walled,

00:01:47.249 --> 00:01:50.049
designed to protect us from the films

00:01:50.049 --> 00:01:51.209
and the films from each other,

00:01:51.209 --> 00:01:54.990
because nitrate film has the
basic same chemical compound

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:58.100
as gunpowder, and is highly flammable.

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:00.789
A nitrate fire is like
nothing you have ever seen.

00:02:00.789 --> 00:02:02.756
Because it's almost like
a controlled explosion.

00:02:02.756 --> 00:02:05.390
It burns with a bright orange-yellow flame

00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:07.509
and it looks like a rocket engine.

00:02:07.509 --> 00:02:09.569
We have negatives in our vaults

00:02:09.569 --> 00:02:11.569
that are over a hundred years old.

00:02:11.569 --> 00:02:12.780
Things come in to us

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:15.449
that have been in barns and warehouses

00:02:15.449 --> 00:02:17.540
and people's attics and basements.

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:18.780
And I've been able to be involved

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:21.340
in the restoration of several films that

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:23.060
otherwise, in the outside world,

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:25.700
would have no monetary value.

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:28.867
But their historical value is immense.

00:02:30.830 --> 00:02:33.430
My interest has always been in early film.

00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:35.584
And I am getting a chance to work with

00:02:35.584 --> 00:02:37.412
one of the largest and
most unique collections

00:02:37.412 --> 00:02:39.043
of early film in the world.

00:02:39.043 --> 00:02:41.543
I think I'm here for a reason.

00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:45.425
I think my purpose

00:02:45.425 --> 00:02:48.763
is to make sure that we remember.

00:02:48.763 --> 00:02:50.872
To know what we had

00:02:50.872 --> 00:02:53.513
and what we looked like and what we did.

00:02:53.513 --> 00:02:56.433
And film is one of the absolute
best ways of doing that.

00:02:56.433 --> 00:02:59.016
(upbeat music)

00:03:01.043 --> 00:03:03.960
(electronic music)

